"""
agent_vars.py — Centralized variables for all LTTM agents and tools.

The values the agent and tool files share live here.
To change any value: edit this file, redeploy (agentcore launch).

IMPORTANT INFORMATION FOR USER - make your own copy and fill it in:
    cp agents/utils/agent_vars.py.example agents/utils/agent_vars.py
Then change every value marked "FILL IN" (search for it):
    - AWS Accounts: the IDs, labels and emails of your three accounts
    - DATALAKE_BUCKET: the same name as `prefix` in terraform/terraform.tfvars
    - USED_REGIONS, IDC_REGION, WORKMAIL_REGION: the regions your accounts use
    - CLOUDTRAIL_LAKE_START, CUR_FIRST_BILLING_PERIOD: where your lake's data starts
    - DNS_EXAMPLE_ZONE_IDS: two of your Route 53 hosted zone IDs, if you log DNS queries
Everything else matches the Terraform in this repository: change it only together with the Terraform.
agent_vars.py itself is git-ignored, so your values stay out of the repository.

Import in any agent or tool:
    import utils.agent_vars as vars
    # then use: vars.ACC1_ID, vars.ACC2_ID, vars.ACCOUNTS, etc.

The values, and why they are what they are:
- ACCOUNTS maps each account ID to its label and email.
- QUERY_TOOLS are the supervisor's sub-agent tools, the query_* wrappers. The integrity hook and the steering judge
  check only these tools' results, so a new sub-agent goes here, and into the supervisor's SUPERVISOR_TOOLS.
- CACHE_NAMES are the names the user sees on the cache lines under the 💰 line, by receipt caller; CACHE_TTL is
  explained in cached_system_prompt.
- ATHENA_WORKGROUP is in DEFAULT_REGION.
- GROUPED_ROW_LIMIT: a query whose own SELECT has a GROUP BY returns one row per group, a total each, so
  SQLRewriteHook lets it return up to this many rows (a list of events stays at 20), and asks Athena for one more,
  so that format_athena_rows can say when there were more groups than it shows.
- MORE_ROWS_KEY: a plain list (no GROUP BY of its own) stays at the hook's 20 rows, and SQLRewriteHook asks Athena
  for one more. When that row comes back, the hook keeps the first 20 and puts this key on the tool result, with
  the number kept; agent_wrapper._extract_raw_results passes it on, and format_athena_rows says that these are not
  all the rows. Strands sends a tool result's content, ID and status to the model, never this key, so the
  sub-agent sees the same 20 rows.
- SESSION_ID_PATTERN: a session ID is a UUID in lower case, as the CLI makes them. AgentCore Runtime takes 33 to
  256 characters, and AgentCore Memory at most 100 letters, digits, "-" and "_". Memory also finds a session's
  summary by its ID as a namespace prefix, next to the facts in "default", so IDs of different lengths, or one
  starting with "default", would mix sessions' memory. The CLI (alexandra.sh) and the stream shim check the same
  rule.
- Regions: every sub-agent that chooses regions takes them from here. USED_REGIONS are the regions the accounts
  actually use; the other regions get only a few AWS-service events a month. Every CloudTrail question searches
  CLOUDTRAIL_REGIONS, unless the user names a region. Sign-ins through the global sign-in page are logged in
  us-east-1, us-east-2, eu-north-1 or, when signing in from Asia-Pacific, ap-southeast-2, so SIGNIN_REGIONS adds
  ap-southeast-2. IDC_REGION (IAM Identity Center) and WORKMAIL_REGION (Amazon WorkMail webmail) are the home
  regions of the two non-console sign-ins in the main account (same three event names).
- The other services' *_REGIONS lists say where each one runs, which is where its sub-agent looks when the user
  names no region; each matches the Terraform resources. CONFIG_REGIONS: the Config recorders, all three accounts;
  CONFIG_GLOBAL_REGION is Config's awsRegion for global resources (IAM), recorded by the home region's recorder.
  GUARDDUTY_REGIONS: the detectors, us-west-2 in the main account only. MACIE_REGIONS, INSPECTOR_REGIONS: enabled
  in all three accounts. ACCESS_ANALYZER_REGIONS: the ORGANIZATION analyzers, in the main account.
  CLOUDWATCH_REGIONS: the log subscriptions, dev and prod in eu-central-1 only. FLOWLOGS_REGIONS: the VPC flow
  logs, us-west-2 in the main account only.
- *_REGIONS_BY_ACCOUNT hold the same per account, where the accounts differ; the lists above are their union. The
  lines written by code into an empty answer name them, so no answer claims a region an account does not cover.
- Where a data source starts, for the same lines: CLOUDTRAIL_LAKE_START, the first CloudTrail files in the lake
  (cloudtrail/, organization trail); CUR_FIRST_BILLING_PERIOD, the first billing period of the cost and usage
  report; HEALTH_RETENTION_DAYS, how long AWS Health keeps events ("up to the 90-day limit", AWS Health User
  Guide, organizational view).
- ACCESS_ANALYZER_NAME: the ORGANIZATION analyzer in the main account, one per region in ACCESS_ANALYZER_REGIONS.
  It covers all three accounts; terraform/access_analyzer.tf creates it.
- GLOBAL_SERVICE_REGION: the region the tools call AWS Organizations and AWS Health in. Quotas of global services
  (QUOTAS_GLOBAL_SERVICES) exist only there, in us-east-1; Service Quotas answers NoSuchResource elsewhere.
- REGION_NAMES: region names a user may type instead of the code. SQLValidatorHook uses them to see which regions
  a question names.
- DATE_BLANKS: the prompts hold the blanks {TODAY}, {YEAR}, {MONTH}, {DAY} and {PREV_YEAR}, filled per question
  (with_current_date). A date computed once at import would go stale in a session that runs past midnight UTC,
  and on AgentCore V2 (one snapshot per deploy) would stay at the deploy day. Every sub-agent fills the blanks
  when it is built for a question, and the supervisor refreshes its prompt for every question.
- TABLES and BLOCKED_SQL_KEYWORDS are the SQL guardrails of sql_validator_hook.py.
- AGENT_LOG_GROUP_PREFIX: the log groups of AgentCore runtimes, LTTM's own included: they hold every user's
  questions, the generated SQL and the internal names. terraform/cloudwatch.tf stops sending them to the lake; the
  CloudWatch agent neither lists nor queries them (lowercase, compared with lowercased names).
- DNS_EXAMPLE_ZONE_IDS: two Route 53 hosted zones whose DNS query logs reach the lake. The CloudWatch agent's
  DNS examples name them; the agent still finds every zone's log group with list_log_groups().
- MAX_RESULT_CHARS: a size limit for results before they reach the supervisor; nothing in agents/ applies it.
- GUARDRAIL_ID and GUARDRAIL_VERSION come from LTTM_GUARDRAIL_ID and LTTM_GUARDRAIL_VERSION, set from the
  terraform outputs guardrail_id and guardrail_version after `terraform apply`. When both are set, every user
  question is checked against the managed guardrail (prompt injection + topic denial) once, when it arrives
  (utils/prompt_guard.py). AGENTCORE_REGION is the region of the AgentCore runtime and its memory; code inside the
  runtime prefers AWS_REGION. GUARDRAIL_REGION: lttm-prompt-guard lives next to the runtime
  (terraform/guardrails.tf).
- INTERNAL_NAMES: the names ArchitectureGuardHook redacts from the model's output; add new tool, hook and plugin
  names here when they are made. The guard matches names in any case and inside words, so every name needs a "_"
  or two capitals: a plain word such as "coverage" turned "full governance coverage" into "full governance
  [internal]", and the bare word "controltower" would match aws-controltower-BaselineCloudTrail,
  aws-controltower/CloudTrailLogs and controltower.amazonaws.com in real answers. Left out for false positives:
  "ACCOUNTS" and "TABLES", and ".py" and "DEFAULT_REGION", which matched tracebacks, handler file names and
  AWS_DEFAULT_REGION in real log data.
"""

